-- title:
--   Unramified ∞-type cusps of X₀(q²M') over X₀(M'), q=3
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime with $q=3$, let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number not divisible by $q$, and let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero that is algebraic over $\mathbb{Q}$. Inside the Laurent series field `LaurentSeries L`, let $K$ be the intermediate field over $L$ given by `laurentBaseChange L` applied to `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ (Gamma0 (q^2 * M'))`, that is, the $L$-subfield generated by the coefficientwise images under `coeffEmb L` of the $\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by quotients $\mathrm{qexp}(f)/\mathrm{qexp}(g)$ of $q$-expansions of modular forms of equal weight for $\Gamma_0(q^2M')$ having integral $q$-expansions; let $K_0$ be the corresponding field for $\Gamma_0(M')$, assumed to satisfy $K_0 \le K$. Let $j \in K$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of `jq`, the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}\cdot(\text{integral power series})$ of the modular invariant, and let $J_2 \in K$ be an element whose Laurent series is the image of `jqN (q^2)`, the substitution $q \mapsto q^{q^2}$ in `jq`. Let $w$ be a place of $K$ over $L$, i.e. a proper valuation subring of $K$ containing $L$ and a principal ideal ring, and write $\operatorname{ord}_w$ for the associated normalised integer valuation. Assume $\operatorname{ord}_w j < 0$ and $\operatorname{ord}_w J_2 = q^2 \cdot \operatorname{ord}_w j$. Then the ramification index of $w$ along the inclusion $K_0 \hookrightarrow K$, namely the least $n > 0$ such that $\operatorname{ord}_w f = n$ for some nonzero $f \in K_0$, equals $1$.
--
--   In the classical language: a cusp of $X_0(q^2M')$ lying on the $\infty$-branch, characterised by $\operatorname{ord}_w j(q^2\tau) = q^2\operatorname{ord}_w j(\tau)$, is unramified in the covering $X_0(q^2M') \to X_0(M')$. This is the $q = 3$ case of the statement, companion to the case $q \ge 5$, and it feeds the analysis of the cusps in terms of the $j$-invariant chart at infinity used further along the semistable-covering argument.
